# PART 1 — CORE SAAS ARCHITECTURE

## 1. Multi-Tenant ISP Platform

Every ISP must have its own isolated account and business environment.

Each ISP must be able to manage:

- Company profile and branding.
- Internet packages and pricing.
- Broadband and PPPoE subscribers.
- Wi-Fi hotspot customers.
- Routers and network zones.
- Payment methods and transactions.
- Invoices and subscriptions.
- Vouchers and authentication.
- Public ISP website.
- Website pages and design.
- Customer portal.
- Support requests.
- Email and notification settings.
- Business reports and authorized subscriber data.

Enforce tenant isolation in the backend and database. An ISP must never access another ISP's private information, router credentials, subscribers, transactions, website drafts or media.

## 2. Separate Platform Administration

Maintain three separate experiences:

1. Public WIFI Pay landing page and demos.
2. ISP business dashboard and customer-facing ISP website.
3. WIFI Pay super-admin dashboard.

The super-admin dashboard must control SaaS plans, trial periods, tenant accounts, domain availability, website-builder settings, system integrations, global templates, feature permissions, support escalation and platform subscription billing.

ISP administrators must not receive super-admin permissions.

Enforce permissions through server-side authorization, not merely by hiding frontend links.

## 3. Production Technology Compatibility

Use the existing project's framework, authentication, database and deployment architecture wherever possible.

Implement reliable database migrations, unique constraints, transactions, audit logs, validation, error handling, backups and rollback procedures.

Store secrets only in secure server-side environment variables or an appropriate secrets manager.

Do not expose database credentials, payment tokens, router passwords or private API keys to the browser.

